            <ArticleTitle>A double-blind study comparing two single-dose regimens of ketorolac with diclofenac in pain due to cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>504-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the analgesic efficacy and safety of two single doses of ketorolac with diclofenac in acute cancer pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Double-blind, randomized, clinical study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Hospital-based clinical research center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred eighty patients suffering acute, moderate, or severe cancer pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A single intramuscular injection of ketorolac 10 or 30 mg or diclofenac 75 mg.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Pain intensity was assessed 30 minutes and 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6 hours after injection or until rescue drug administration. In approximately 70% of patients all treatments provided prompt sustained pain relief throughout the 6-hour observation period. There were no statistically significant differences in any of the analyzed efficacy measures among the three groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Intramuscular ketorolac 10 mg is adequate to relieve cancer pain, and is equivalent to ketorolac 30 mg and to diclofenac 75 mg.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
